==Accessory / Trinket==
* Damage, cooldown reduction, or defense should be prioritized.
* Buff synergy is more important than base stats in dungeons.

==Stat Setup (Dungeon Rules)==
* Player damage is treated as having max stats in all categories.
* Dungeon buffs should focus on:
** Damage increases
** Weapon-specific boosts
** Defense and sustain

'''''Note on Difficulty Scaling'''''

The build recommendations above are not officially split by difficulty (Normal/Hard/Challenge). In practice, survivability-focused buffs and cards (Fortress, Unbreakable, Lifesteal) become more important at higher difficulties, since Challenge-mode enemies can defeat players in a single hit. 

==How to Play the Build==
===Early Rooms (1–4)===
# The player should use [[Buddha]] with Sword M1 attacks to clear rooms as quickly as possible.
#* A strong [[Swords|sword]] or [[Guns|gun]] should be used regardless of the player's usual choice, since the mode is not PvP and prioritizes [[Area of Effect|AoE]] and damage over [[stun]] and combo potential.
#* If there are no [[Buddha]] users, the remaining players can use all the attacks of their [[Swords|Sword]], [[Guns|Gun]], [[Fighting Styles|Fighting Style]], and [[Blox Fruits|Blox Fruit]] in succession.

===Boss Rooms (5 / 10 / 15)===
# Buffs that help in boss fights, such as Lifesteal, stat increases, Unbreakable, and Fortress, should be prioritized.
#* Crowd control from [[Lightning]] or [[Dough]] can help control the boss.
#* [[Buddha]] or [[Dragon]] users should handle burst [[Area of Effect|AoE]] damage, and the team should deal as much damage as possible while the boss is stunned.
#* If a [[Gas]] user is present, they can stun the boss continuously with M1 moves. The other players should avoid disturbing the stun and focus on M1 attacks or skills without knockback.

'''Late Rooms:'''
* Buff stacking provides significant damage and survivability.
* Damage buffs should be prioritized over raw [[Stats|stat]] increases.
* Cooldown reduction buffs are especially useful in Challenge difficulty.

==Tips==
* During the Shrine and Vent phase of [[Kitsune Lord]] and [[Gas Knight]], the user can use a long-range ability (e.g. Explosive Gun M1) below the text to instantly kill it. This also works with non-Explosive guns for the vents, though the player may need to move closer or be at the same height as the vent.
* Clearing the Blocks of [[Zero]] does not do much, since they deal relatively low damage; killing the boss quickly should be a higher priority.
* Storm Blessing or an [[Enchantments|enchantment]] such as Vampiric (or Curse of the Reaper) will greatly increase the chance of survivability. Siphon is also particularly useful if the player runs out of [[energy]] frequently.
* Each player focusing on one side allows four players to cover all four sides of the dungeon room, which speeds up completion significantly.
* [[Buddha]] users with [[Dragon Trident]] should have Rage [[Enchantments]] to gain speed.
* Using [[Gas]] is recommended for completing dungeons.
* Using [[Gas]]'s X move (Bursting Vapor) at the Kitsune Shrine and on the [[Kitsune Lord]] simultaneously during its transformation can deal very high damage, sometimes removing half of its health on Normal difficulty.

==Notes==
* Dying in the dungeon has no effect as of now. The player respawns after five seconds and is able to continue the dungeon as normal.
* During the fight in the dungeon, [[items]]/[[inventory]], [[Shop|shops]], and stats are disabled. This means that the user cannot switch, equip, or use items, or switch fruits from the shop. The player should equip their best build before entering the dungeon.
* When using fruits that have a transformation that cancels [[Fighting Styles]], [[Swords]], and [[Guns]] and has no Fury meter, the player does not need to consider which [[Swords]] or [[Guns]] to bring, and can store them where they will not be seen. However, since the player has max stats on Sword, Gun, Melee, and Blox Fruit in the dungeon, playing untransformed is recommended so the player can take advantage of their whole build.
* The player has a maximum of 50 energy (not to be confused with the [[Energy]] bar; this is dungeon-exclusive), which refills to maximum every eight hours. The player cannot start a dungeon if they run out of energy. Doing one dungeon uses 10 energy, and the player regains one energy every nine minutes and 36 seconds. On completing a Normal dungeon, it may appear that the dungeon actually took eight or nine energy, since the player would have gained one or two energy while doing the dungeon.
* While clearing room 10 or room 15, all players must destroy the shrines/vents to stop the boss from healing; if not destroyed, the boss can heal to max HP or explode the whole floor, killing everyone on it.
* Each difficulty has a requirement to play: Normal is unlocked by default, Hard requires 200 [[Simulation Data]] to unlock, and Challenge requires 1,000 [[Simulation Data]] to unlock.
* Upon entering the dungeon, all of the user's [[stats]] are set to max even if they have a low [[Levels|level]] (e.g. Lv. 1550). However, the user still needs to be at least [[Levels|Lv.]] 1100 to enter the dungeon.
* Challenge dungeons contain reward tiers, such as the Gold Reward Tier or Platinum Reward Tier. Higher tiers offer better rewards, stronger trinkets, and an increased chance of receiving three trinkets from a single dungeon.
* In the dungeon, the player's [[Race Awakening|Race Meter]] fills up faster when taking damage from enemy NPCs than when dealing damage to them. Players can take advantage of this if they have a Vampiric/Curse of the Reaper weapon, or have received the Overflow/Lifesteal buff earlier in the dungeon, since they can sustain their V4 for extended periods and clear dungeons more easily.

